{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CesvhbGeZj4gT79vEbYc8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The repo has an empty `intent/`, so this becomes `intent/0001-<slug>/intent.md`. I've noted the conversation start (`2026-09-09T13:14:51Z`) for the PR body.\n\nBefore I write it: the file wants observed facts and your words, not my guesses. A round of questions:\n\n**Problem (the facts I'd record)**\n1. \"A third of every call\" — where does that come from? A measured sample, a support lead's estimate, ticket tags? And roughly how many calls/week are we talking about?\n2. What do customers actually get told? Is it a status word (\"in review\"), a date, an explanation of a delay — or does support have to go dig somewhere before they can answer?\n\n**The problem under the solution**\n3. You've said \"see it in the portal.\" Is the underlying problem *customers have no way to learn claim status without calling*, or something narrower — e.g. they can see something today but it's stale/unintelligible? If the portal specifically is a hard requirement rather than the obvious route, I'll record it as a constraint instead.\n\n**Scope**\n4. All claim types, or one line of business first?\n5. Status only, or also the things people usually ask in the same breath — what's needed from them next, expected timeline, documents received?\n\n**Users and systems**\n6. Which portal is this, and where does claim status live today (which system does support look at)?\n7. Anyone beyond support and customers who should be in the room — claims operations, security/privacy, whoever owns the portal?\n\n**Constraints and success**\n8. Anything that must hold? I'd guess existing portal login only and no new personal data exposed, but tell me if there's a regulatory angle (what you're allowed to show a customer mid-claim) or a rule about not showing internal states.\n9. Finish this so you can check it in six months: \"This worked if _____